  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.title": "Scripts, Genres & Formats",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.p1": "Format is the production shape, such as movie, series, live action, animated feature, or anime film.",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.p2": "Genre is the audience promise, such as drama, action, biopic, sports, documentary, musical, animation, anime, or similar lanes.",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.p3": "Primary genre carries the main identity. Secondary genre adds flavor and should support the project.",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.tag1": "Format = package",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.tag2": "Genre = promise",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.tag3": "Tone = feel",
  "guide.handbook.scriptFormats.tag4": "Audience = reach",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.title": "Greenlight Flow",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.p1": "Greenlight turns a script into a production plan with director, cast, crew, gear, locations, tone, and final package.",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.p2": "Budget is not just a number. Talent fees, crew quality, production choices, known characters, and scale all move it.",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.p3": "Negotiation matters. Returning cast and bigger names can be powerful, but they still need deals.",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.b1": "Use the cast step to lock who plays each role.",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.b2": "Use connected characters when the story belongs to a franchise or universe.",
  "guide.handbook.greenlight.b3": "Use the review step to catch weak spots before committing.",
